Another idea is that the issue might be because of a non-atomic transition in conn_destroy() from BT_CONN_DISCONNECTING to BT_CONN_DISCONNECTED. See this:

```
static void conn_destroy(struct bt_conn *conn, void *data)
{
	if (conn->state == BT_CONN_CONNECTED ||
	    conn->state == BT_CONN_DISCONNECTING) {
		bt_conn_set_state(conn, BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE);
	}

	bt_conn_set_state(conn, BT_CONN_DISCONNECTED);
}
```

Hi @jhedberg 

I spent some time debugging the issue and now fully understand the problem. 
So first thing - my idea regarding conn_destroy() function was wrong, as the function is used when bt_disable() only.

Let me then describe the scenario. We have central which is iOS-based, and peripheral which is Zephyr-based:
1. central sends WRITE REQUEST to the characteristic
2. central without waiting for the response sends LL_TERMINATE_IND to terminate the connection
3. peripheral handles WRITE REQUEST and prepares WRITE RESPONSE. Because the connection state is BT_CONN_CONNECTED the WRITE RESPONSE is stored in the TX queue
4. as a result of LL_TERMINATE_IND  HCI event is received in bt_ipm_rx_thread (BT_HCI_EVT_DISCONN_COMPLETE), and it is handled by hci_disconn_complete_prio() changing the state of BLE connection to BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE
5. now hci_tx_thread takes its role and tries to send WRITE RESPONSE. As the connection state is BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE, in the bt_conn_process_tx() function clean-up is not performed, see this (it is performed in BT_CONN_DISCONNECTED  only)

```
void bt_conn_process_tx(struct bt_conn *conn)
{
	struct net_buf *buf;
	int err;

	LOG_DBG("conn %p", conn);

	if (conn->state == BT_CONN_DISCONNECTED &&
	    atomic_test_and_clear_bit(conn->flags, BT_CONN_CLEANUP)) {
		LOG_DBG("handle %u disconnected - cleaning up", conn->handle);
		conn_cleanup(conn);
		return;
	}
```

But when it comes to:
```
	/* Since we used `peek`, the queue still owns the reference to the
	 * buffer, so we need to take an explicit additional reference here.
	 */
	buf = net_buf_ref(buf);
	err = send_buf(conn, buf);
	net_buf_unref(buf);

	if (err  == -EIO) {
		struct bt_conn_tx *tx = tx_data(buf)->tx;
```
send_buf() functions requires the connection state to be BT_CONN_CONNECTED , and if not it returns -ENOTCONN (-128).
This ends up not releasing net buffer holding the WRITE RESPONSE message.

Does it make sense to you? I did a patch and it seems to me this solves the problem. At the beginning of bt_conn_process_tx() the if statement to clean up has been modified to:
```
	bool disconnecting = conn->state == BT_CONN_DISCONNECTED ||
		conn->state == BT_CONN_DISCONNECTING ||
		conn->state == BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE;

	if (disconnecting &&
	    atomic_test_and_clear_bit(conn->flags, BT_CONN_CLEANUP)) {
		LOG_INF("handle %u disconnected - cleaning up", conn->handle);
		conn_cleanup(conn);
		return;
	}
```

and for send_buf() error handling:
```
	/* Since we used `peek`, the queue still owns the reference to the
	 * buffer, so we need to take an explicit additional reference here.
	 */
	buf = net_buf_ref(buf);
	err = send_buf(conn, buf);
	net_buf_unref(buf);

	if (err  == -EIO || err == -ENOTCONN) {
		struct bt_conn_tx *tx = tx_data(buf)->tx;
```

It works for me, but I am not sure if this is the proper solution.

Looking at the connection state machine I found something other, which looks for me a little bit weird. I don't understand the transition in hci_core.c - bt_hci_le_enh_conn_complete():
```
	bt_conn_set_state(conn, BT_CONN_CONNECTED);

	if (is_disconnected) {
		/* Mark the connection as already disconnected before calling
		 * the connected callback, so that the application cannot
		 * start sending packets
		 */
		bt_conn_set_state(conn, BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE);
	}

	bt_conn_connected(conn);

	/* Start auto-initiated procedures */
	conn_auto_initiate(conn);
```
Is the transition from BT_CONN_CONNECTED to BT_CONN_DISCONNECT_COMPLETE proper?

